MEDIA = {
'Catholic': 'Vatican II\'s Inter Mirifica (1963), Communio et Progressio (1971) and "Ethics in Communications" (2000) hold that media should serve truth, dignity and the common good. Depicting evil is acceptable when it serves understanding and is not made attractive (Inter Mirifica 7). John Paul II\'s Letter to Artists (1999) praises art that seeks beauty. CCC 2493-2499 and 2525 address media and permissiveness.',
'Eastern Orthodox': 'Guarding the senses (nepsis, watchfulness) is a core practice: what one looks at shapes the soul. Holy images are venerated, so mocking icons or sacred things is serious. Fathers such as John Chrysostom warned against the theatre. "Bases of the Social Concept" (2000) asks media to respect moral values.',
'Baptist': 'Philippians 4:8 ("whatever is true... pure... lovely") is the common test. SBC resolutions have criticized entertainment content (for example the 1997 Disney boycott). Soul liberty means each family decides, and Plugged In is widely used.',
'Non-denominational Evangelical': 'Philippians 4:8 and Romans 14 (freedom and conscience) guide choices. Many distinguish depicting sin from endorsing it and speak of "engaging" or "redeeming" culture; others favor separation. Plugged In and Christianity Today reviews are common references.',
'Pentecostal / Charismatic': 'Media can be an "open door" to spiritual influence, so occult and demonic content is weighed more heavily than in many traditions. Holiness standards (modesty, separation from worldliness) vary by church.',
'Reformed / Presbyterian': 'Common grace (Kuyper) lets families value good art by non-Christians. Westminster Larger Catechism Q139 lists "lascivious songs, books, pictures... stage plays" among sins against the seventh commandment. The second commandment (Heidelberg Catechism Q96-98) makes images of God or Christ a concern for many.',
'Lutheran': 'Entertainment is generally adiaphora (neither commanded nor forbidden), judged by love and conscience. Luther valued art and music. Confessional (LCMS, WELS) and mainline (ELCA) families differ in strictness.',
'Methodist / Wesleyan': 'Wesley\'s General Rules ask members to avoid "diversions as cannot be used in the name of the Lord Jesus" and books that do not "tend to the knowledge or love of God." Holiness churches keep this caution; the United Methodist Church is much more open today.',
'Anglican / Episcopal': 'A strong literary tradition (C.S. Lewis, Dorothy Sayers) sees imagination as a gift. Works are judged by how they frame good and evil rather than by what they show. Practice ranges from progressive to conservative.',
'Anabaptist (Mennonite, Amish, Brethren)': 'Separation from the world (Schleitheim Confession, Article 4) leads Old Order groups to avoid TV and most popular media. Mennonite Church USA is much more open. Violence shown as heroic is the central concern across the range.',
'Latter-day Saint': 'For the Strength of Youth (2022) asks youth to choose entertainment that invites the Spirit, replacing earlier specific counsel (such as avoiding R-rated films) with principles. Article of Faith 13 seeks what is "virtuous, lovely, or of good report."',
'Mainline progressive Protestant': 'No formal media rules. Works are engaged critically, with attention to representation, justice and how the vulnerable are treated.',
'Jewish': 'Lashon hara (harmful speech), nivul peh (vulgar speech) and tzniut (modesty) shape judgments. Many Orthodox and especially Haredi communities limit secular entertainment and internet use; Conservative and Reform families decide by values. Antisemitic tropes are a concern across the range.',
'Muslim': 'Scholars differ on images of living beings; depicting prophets (including Jesus and Moses as prophets), God or angels is widely forbidden. Music is debated. "Lowering the gaze" (Qur\'an 24:30-31) shapes views on immodest content, and some read 31:6 ("idle talk") as caution about entertainment.',
'Hindu': 'Classical aesthetics (rasa theory, Natya Shastra) values art that evokes emotion. Deities are freely depicted, but mockery or misuse of them is objected to. Some apply the Gita\'s sattvic, rajasic and tamasic qualities (chapter 17) to media.',
'Buddhist': 'Right speech and mindfulness of what feeds the mind guide choices. On observance days lay people often take the eight precepts, which include abstaining from shows and entertainment. Monastics avoid entertainment.',
'Secular progressive': 'No religious rules. Judgments focus on representation, consent, stereotypes and age-appropriateness (Common Sense Media, AAP guidance).',
'Secular humanist': 'Free inquiry and opposition to censorship are valued, with critical thinking about what is shown. Concern centers on cruelty, exploitation and pseudoscience presented as fact.',
'Conservative / traditional': 'The classical ideal of "the good, the true and the beautiful" guides choices, with concern about media that undermines family, faith, duty or national tradition.',
'Seventh-day Adventist': 'Fundamental Belief 22 asks members to choose entertainment "of the highest quality" and avoid what is impure. Ellen White warned against fiction and theatre that excite the passions; "by beholding we become changed" (2 Corinthians 3:18) is a common principle.',
"Jehovah's Witness": 'jw.org counsels asking whether entertainment features what God hates, especially violence (Psalm 11:5), immorality and spiritism (Galatians 5:19-21). Many choices are left to conscience, but magic and occult content is widely avoided.',
'Christian Science': 'Thought shapes experience, so members guard what they dwell on ("Stand porter at the door of thought," Science and Health 392). Depictions of fear, disease and sin are seen as mental influences to be guarded against.',
'Unitarian Universalist': 'No formal media rules. The principles (inherent worth and dignity, justice, free search for truth) are applied, with concern about stereotypes and attention to age-appropriateness.',
'Wiccan / Pagan': 'No central rule. The Rede ("harm none") guides choices, and the main concern is accurate, respectful portrayals of witches and pagans.',
'Shinto': 'No doctrine on fiction. Kami and yokai are common in Japanese popular culture and generally accepted. Concerns center on disrespect toward shrines and kami; sincerity, purity and gratitude are the values applied.',
'Sikh': 'The Rehat Maryada does not address fiction. Depicting the Gurus in images or film is opposed by the SGPC. The five vices (lust, anger, greed, attachment, ego) shown as admirable are a concern.',
"Bahá'í": 'Arts are praised as worship when done in service. Shoghi Effendi\'s call to a "chaste and holy life" warns against degrading art, literature and entertainment (to verify exact wording).',
'Scientologist': 'No content code for fiction. The Way to Happiness precepts and the Church\'s opposition to psychiatry are the main lenses.',
'Satanist': 'No content code. Art that challenges authority is valued (Milton\'s Satan as heroic rebel). Concerns are harm to children and animals (Eleven Rules) and stereotyped depictions of Satanists.',
}
